db/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162225 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186163049 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162225 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186163050 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162225 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186163051 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162225 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186163052 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162225 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186163056 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162225 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162225 #edb/ref 1045 "The Singular Values of a Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162225 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186163048 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162225 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162225 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162225 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "af1642f1-edfe-5e8b-bb3e-376bc11c5af4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162361 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268802 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162361 #edb/ref 1069 "q-139418" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162361 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162361 #edb/ref 1071 "A $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=10$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=2\\sqrt{5},$ find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162361 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cdd91aaf-b8dd-5936-93a2-ee3b24ba20f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162361 #edb/ref 1074 "The matrix $A$ maps the unit circle ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto the ellipse ${Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is ${\\sigma }_{1}=10,$ the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ must be ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}={10}^{2}.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax{‖}^{2}}={10}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax‖}=10.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is ${\\sigma }_{1}=10.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162364 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186159386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162364 #edb/ref 1069 "q-139491" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162364 #edb/ref 1071 "A $2 \\cdot 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x \\mid ‖x‖ = 1}$ from $R^{3}$ onto an ellipse in $R^{2}$. Given that the eigenvalues of $A^{T}A$ are $7$, $3$, and $0$, find the length of the ellipse's semi-major axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162364 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7f4eb209-024e-54c1-8d3d-a6d7fb7fadd8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163048 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163049 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163048 #edb/ref 1061 37249 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163048 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "62510b93-1090-5902-b6a0-45fc7a644d01" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163048 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163315 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163049 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163052 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163049 #edb/ref 1061 37250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163049 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "01d42207-c2b3-55a2-9d11-6fcda35c9cca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163049 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162097 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163050 #edb/ref 1061 37251 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163050 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bc576dbc-6ac4-5a02-9348-f4cf6db1eb0c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163050 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162098 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163051 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163050 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163051 #edb/ref 1061 37254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163051 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "94acc5c3-ff1a-5747-b32d-b0db6e46436e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163051 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162099 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163052 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163056 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163052 #edb/ref 1061 37255 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163052 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ef1b9251-7972-5caa-8ac8-321830fa4c27" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163052 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163316 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163056 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163051 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163056 #edb/ref 1061 37280 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163056 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c9015782-c2cb-5d34-8e6d-84a7dd5678d9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163056 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163320 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163315 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's suppose we are given the matrix\n\n![](images/18/18f57c05cb79918e17401427e3419648791562a5d38116582e0d396e20db1069.svg)\n\nThen, the corresponding linear transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf x \\mapsto A \\mathbf x\n$$\n\nmaps the unit circle $\\big\\{ \\mathbf x \\, | \\, \\|\\mathbf x\\|=1 \\big\\}$ in $\\mathbb R^2$ onto an ellipse $\\big\\{ A\\mathbf{x} \\,|\\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ in $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/c1/c163f7714d0e2045db37438b7ea66a2dc8ac92cfbe309a0d24c898bbfac8c295.png)\n\n \n\nHow can we find the maximum possible value of $\\|A \\mathbf x\\|?$ Geometrically, this is equivalent to finding the semi-major axis of our ellipse.\n\n![](images/86/866052291b0d7a416988c0c42fa99eec1d1bfd3b1344cf3cf2eb0a09457ae38d.png)\n\nFirst, we note the following:\n\n- The value of $\\mathbf x$ that maximizes $||A\\mathbf x||$ also maximizes $||A\\mathbf x||^2.$\n\n- If $\\mathbf v$ is a column vector, then $||\\mathbf v||^2 = \\mathbf v^T \\mathbf v\\mathbin{:}$\n\nTherefore, we can re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $A\\mathbf{x}$ on our ellipse as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNote that $\\mathbf{x}^T (A^T A) \\mathbf{x}$ is a quadratic form with the matrix $A^T A.$ Therefore, the maximum value of this quadratic form for $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1$ equals the largest eigenvalue of the matrix $A^T A.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the eigenvalues of the matrix $A^TA\\mathbin{:}$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}10-\\lambda  & 6 \\\\ 6 & 10-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (10-\\lambda )^{2}-36 & =0 \\\\ (10-\\lambda )^{2} & =36 \\\\ 10-\\lambda  & =\\pm 6 \\\\ \\lambda  & =4,\\,16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the largest eigenvalue of $A^T A$ is $16,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\max\\limits_{\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1} \\big\\{ \\| A\\mathbf{x} \\|^2 \\big\\} = 16\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\max\\limits_{\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1} \\big\\{ \\| A\\mathbf{x} \\| \\big\\} = \\sqrt{16} = 4.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the length of the semi-major axis of our ellipse equals $4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163315 #edb/ref 1065 6133 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163315 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b6f73158-b78c-5262-bb9d-a957e3f79fa3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163315 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163316 #edb/ref 1064 "Let $A$ be an $m\\times n$ matrix. Then, $A^T A$ is always a symmetric, positive semi-definite matrix. In particular, $A^TA$ can be orthogonally diagonalized, and its eigenvalues are non-negative real numbers.\n\nThe **singular values** of  $A$ are defined as the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix $A^T A.$ The $i$th singular value of $A$ is denoted $\\sigma_i\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- $\\sigma_1 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_1}$\n\n- $\\sigma_2 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_2}$\n\n- $\\sigma_3 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_3}$\n\n$\\:\\vdots$\n\n- $\\sigma_n = \\sqrt{\\lambda_n}$\n\nWe usually list the singular values in *decreasing* order. \n\nNow, we know that $A^TA$ is orthogonally diagonalizable. Therefore, if\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\mathbf v_1, \\mathbf v_2\\,\\ldots, \\mathbf v_n\\right\\}\n$$\n\nis an *orthonormal* basis of $\\mathbb R^n$ consisting of the eigenvectors of ${A^T}{A}$ with the corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1\\geq \\lambda_2\\geq \\ldots \\geq \\lambda_n,\n$$\n\nthen it can be shown that\n\n- $\\sigma_1 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_1}$ is the length of $A\\mathbf v_1,$\n\n- $\\sigma_2 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_2}$ is the length of $A\\mathbf v_2,$\n\n$\\:\\vdots$\n\n- $\\sigma_n = \\sqrt{\\lambda_n}$ is the length of $A\\mathbf v_n.$\n\nFurthermore:\n\n- $\\sigma_1$ is the maximum value of $||A\\mathbf x||$ over all *unit* vectors $\\mathbf x,$ and is attained on the unit eigenvector $\\mathbf v_1$\n\n- $\\sigma_2$ is the maximum value of $||A\\mathbf x||$ over all *unit* vectors $\\mathbf x$ that are orthogonal to $\\mathbf v_1,$ and is attained on the unit eigenvector $\\mathbf v_2$\n\n- $\\sigma_3$ is the maximum value of $||A\\mathbf x||$ over all *unit* vectors $\\mathbf x$ that are orthogonal to $\\mathbf v_1$ *and* $\\mathbf v_2,$ and is attained on the unit eigenvector $\\mathbf v_3$\n\n$\\:\\vdots$\n\n- $\\sigma_n$ is the maximum value of $||A\\mathbf x||$ over all *unit* vectors $\\mathbf x$ that are orthogonal to $\\mathbf v_1,\\mathbf v_2,\\ldots,\\mathbf v_{n-1},$ and is attained on the unit eigenvector $\\mathbf v_n$\n\nLet's take a look at a concrete example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163316 #edb/ref 1065 6134 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163316 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f470bdb6-fa79-595c-beba-f8242d4426bd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163316 #edb/ref 1067 "The Singular Values of a Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163320 #edb/ref 1064 "We've seen that for the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n![](images/9c/9c11e176876a767543250d5b18038cae7b14a36a692a6f14474da6b4312a48b9.svg)\n\nthe eigenvalues of $A^TA$ are $\\lambda_1=16$ and $\\lambda_2=4.$ It's easy to show that the corresponding unit eigenvectors of $A^T A$ are \n\n![](images/7c/7c44b67b766ea8385f4d7780523ee560bd3e215a0cee42db9baf208da471ccdd.svg) \n\nThe singular values of $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{16}=4, \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{4}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting all of this information together gives us the following diagram. \n\n![](images/23/238f4ae60c783dd786476a339f012ef1b4ca82bcac7aa3f3ef4445726938ef3a.png)\n\n \n\nNote that our ellipse's semi-major and semi-minor axes are $\\sigma_1 = 4$ and $\\sigma_2 =2,$ respectively." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163320 #edb/ref 1065 6139 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163320 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a319c696-fd06-5e8f-99f1-26ad08271a27" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163320 #edb/ref 1067 "Associating Singular Values With an Ellipse's Principal Axes" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266285 #edb/ref 1080 "10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266285 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266285 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "10e0290b-b214-51b9-ae5b-fc3e291433d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266326 #edb/ref 1080 "5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266326 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266326 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "157ee7b9-2a37-56af-a675-3b93033c19d0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266463 #edb/ref 1080 "10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266463 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759218