f 17592186045417] [17592186268705 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268705 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186266858 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268705 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a82d9035-63ea-54b8-90da-3fbf50bd2ad3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268802 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186266691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268802 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186266747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268802 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186267407 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268802 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186268006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268802 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186268347 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268802 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268802 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268802 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186268347 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268802 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "dfe6c093-26dc-5d7a-ada3-01470001afa3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269230 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268451 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269230 #edb/ref 1069 "q-139492" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269230 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269230 #edb/ref 1071 "A $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are $12,$ $9,$ and $0,$ find the length of the ellipse's semi-major axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269230 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e48008ac-4acd-5d33-8b94-d523fa814086" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269230 #edb/ref 1074 "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto the ellipse ${Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$\n\nSince the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ is $12,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax{‖}^{2}}=12\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax‖}=\\sqrt{12}=2\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is $2\\sqrt{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269236 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268705 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269236 #edb/ref 1069 "q-139526" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269236 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269236 #edb/ref 1071 "A $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are $15,$ $10,$ and $0,$ find the length of the ellipse's semi-major axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269236 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "72c2ac9a-8e8a-51d7-84bb-f51632a65f59" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269236 #edb/ref 1074 "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto the ellipse ${Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$\n\nSince the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ is $15,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax{‖}^{2}}=15\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax‖}=\\sqrt{15}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is $\\sqrt{15}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269241 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268570 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269241 #edb/ref 1069 "q-139541" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269241 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269241 #edb/ref 1071 "A $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=5,$ ${\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{2},$ and ${\\sigma }_{3}=0,$ find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269241 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a90de0de-8088-5773-8834-5a0e4d9710cb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269241 #edb/ref 1074 "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto the ellipse ${Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is ${\\sigma }_{1}=5,$ the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ must be ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}={5}^{2}.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax{‖}^{2}}={5}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax‖}=5.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is ${\\sigma }_{1}=5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269242 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268577 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269242 #edb/ref 1069 "q-139542" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269242 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269242 #edb/ref 1071 "A $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=6,$ ${\\sigma }_{2}=4,$ and ${\\sigma }_{3}=0,$ find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269242 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ce6f50bf-ae89-57dc-a707-1d28279d986e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269242 #edb/ref 1074 "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto the ellipse ${Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is ${\\sigma }_{1}=6,$ the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ must be ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}={6}^{2}.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax{‖}^{2}}={6}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax‖}=6.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is ${\\sigma }_{1}=6.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269245 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268512 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269245 #edb/ref 1069 "q-139548" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269245 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269245 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/6b/6be1f51600ae7b42ad0c4c8f67eb8f3ba3570606ec4e3e5e75c65df397aab060.png)\n\nA $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A,$ what are the nonzero singular values of $A?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269245 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bfc4c8a3-4cfa-58b3-bd85-7a7059d385d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269245 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let \n\n$$\n{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\,…,{v}_{n}}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of ${R}^{n}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A$ with corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}\\ge …\\ge {\\lambda }_{n}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{1},$\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $||A{v}_{1}||=7={\\sigma }_{1},$ and\n\n- $||A{v}_{2}||=5={\\sigma }_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the nonzero singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=7$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269246 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268634 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269246 #edb/ref 1069 "q-139555" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269246 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269246 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/2d/2d1cefc0c789518d21279c5c49a18f80220a2aad80973f0e84fda0ddaf25493b.png)\n\nA $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A,$ which of the following is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $100$ of ${A}^{T}A?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269246 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5f621452-f60c-5e1d-b4df-620aa05fd780" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269246 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let \n\n$$\n{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\,…,{v}_{n}}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of ${R}^{n}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A$ with corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}\\ge …\\ge {\\lambda }_{n}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{1},$\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $||A{v}_{1}||=10={\\sigma }_{1},$ which means that ${\\lambda }_{1}={10}^{2}=100,$ and\n\n- $||A{v}_{2}||=8={\\sigma }_{2},$ which means that ${\\lambda }_{2}={8}^{2}=64.$\n\nTherefore, an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=100$ of ${A}^{T}A$ is ${v}_{1}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369748 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186369749 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369748 #edb/ref 1069 "q-139553" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369748 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369748 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/f9/f9b7a55ee1b8d3327ae9da740feaafde459daaee4e42a27cf26fd0ac8e63f20b.png)\n\nA $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A,$ which of the following is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $36$ of ${A}^{T}A?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369748 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "17f11a2f-af39-5dc3-9819-9bfbdefde21e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369748 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let \n\n$$\n{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\,…,{v}_{n}}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of ${R}^{n}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A$ with corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}\\ge …\\ge {\\lambda }_{n}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{1},$\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $||A{v}_{1}||=6={\\sigma }_{1},$ which means that ${\\lambda }_{1}={6}^{2}=36,$ and\n\n- $||A{v}_{2}||=2={\\sigma }_{2},$ which means that ${\\lambda }_{2}={2}^{2}=4.$\n\nTherefore, an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=36$ of ${A}^{T}A$ is ${v}_{1}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369749 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186369750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369749 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186369751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369749 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186369752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369749 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186369753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369749 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186369754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369749 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369749 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369749 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186369750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369749 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "0b2343b2-0201-5c51-9b20-71321c6397b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369750 #edb/ref 1080 "{v}_{1}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369750 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369750 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e5508c57-b781-54c4-ac3c-ea5f7e99fa1f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369751 #edb/ref 1080 "{v}_{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369751 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369751 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9ad78cd4-8bf5-5788-bd3c-9fdda0f21f36" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369752 #edb/ref 1080 "6{v}_{1}+2{v}_{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369752 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369752 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9ec95e10-23b1-53ad-b883-d230280ba441" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369753 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369753 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369753 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6bc9be44-ee1f-5d95-aa5a-ac6bdff702eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369754 #edb/ref 1080 "6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369754 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369754 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e55ed1bd-18b5-554f-9082-1e45710378cb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186383424 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186383425 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186383424 #edb/ref 1069 "q-139540" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186383424 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186383424 #edb/ref 1071 "A $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=7,$ ${\\sigma }_{2}=2\\sqrt{3},$ and ${\\sigma }_{3}=0,$ find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186383424 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b5c458b4-8436-5a2e-a1dd-0c56afc161a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186383424 #edb/ref 1074 "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto the ellipse ${Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is ${\\sigma }_{1}=7,$ the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ must be ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}={7}^{2}.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax{‖}^{2}}={7}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax‖}=7.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is ${\\sigma }_{1}=7.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186383425 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186383426 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186383425 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186383427 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186383425 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186383428 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759218638